# keel

NAS appliance. Web GUI. Speed and low overhead beat feature count.

- Do not add Electron, TUI admin, or a plugin marketplace without an explicit ask.
- Keep the daemon one process. Measure RSS and API latency when changing the hot path.
- Linux is the real NAS storage stack. Windows mock inventory is for developing the GUI on this PC; a Windows NAS host is a separate backend, not ZFS-on-Linux.
- Install onto an existing OS over SSH/WinRM. Do not assume mDNS or same-subnet magic.
- Short forms in the UI (`lsblk`, RSS) are Guide links (terms.js), not unexplained jargon.
- GUI copy is plain words and a proposal (Use this / Leave this). Do not put machine names (`vm-data`, `vmbr`, `local-lvm`) in headings by default. Optional: show the names that computer uses. Do not invent clever metaphors.
- Look around reads names from the other computer after sign-in. It does not invent Proxmox labels. Everyday keel uses share names. Proxmox listing is keep-existing-files (this lab now; others mainly at install).
- UI interaction follows Cerebro (`memory-bank/ui.md`): window is the frame; sidebar never scrolls; extras open from a menu to the right (`This computer`: Disks, Install, Repair); Guide/Settings/Sign in above Notes; Notes at the bottom of the sidebar; Save-button rule; errors name the fix; not-running is grey not red. Do not copy Cerebro’s yellow accent — Keel stays sea-glass. Chrome lives in `web/chrome.js` plus flyout CSS in `web/style.css`.
- The window is the frame: do not make the page scroll. One `.scroll` region per view; Why boxes start closed. Notes is a list + pad split.
- Install progress is one sentence and a bar. Do not dump helper stdout into the GUI. The helper writes `/run/keel/install.json`; the page polls `GET /api/installer/progress`. The GitHub token is saved in Settings after install, never baked into the ISO.
- Notes persist under `data/` (gitignored). Do not treat Notes as family file storage.
- Never put remote passwords in chat transcripts you control, memory-bank, or git. Sign-in is the Pools page.
- Ship the rok-inspired service catalog (including guests that were stopped on rok). Do not add a third-party plugin bazaar.
- Catalog is native systemd units. Do not add containers to save idle CPU; stop the unit instead. Installed ≠ running: open an app when you need it.
- Overlay (Tailscale today; swap the brand, not the job) stays up so files and the GUI work away from home. Never stop it to save watts.
- House-network file access is Samba. Guest-only shares fail on current Windows, so Live uses name `keel` and password `keel` on purpose until real sign-in. Do not expose that on the public internet.
- Proxmox/TrueNAS passwords stay in the GUI. Tickets for listing files stay in daemon memory only, never in data/ or git.
- Update signing key: public half in `packaging/updates/release-public.hex`, private half only in the GitHub secret `KEEL_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY`. Never write the private key into git, memory-bank, or chat. Program updates restore the previous binary if the new one does not answer `/api/health`. OS slot updates stay RAUC; do not hand-roll a second root.
- The GitHub repo is private. Settings Connect GitHub uses device flow (code on the Keel screen, approve on a phone or PC). The access token is saved to `KEEL_DATA/github.token` and must never be returned from an API or committed. The GitHub app id is public (`packaging/updates/github-client-id`, `KEEL_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID`, or `KEEL_DATA/github.client_id`). Never commit a client secret.
